---
title: "Documentation translation"
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

Translation contributions should target the usage documentation, so that
people can learn to use NAHPU in their preferred language. This includes the
usage guides, how-to recipes, best practices, and the Day One guide. Contributor
guidance, including this Contributing section, stays in English, mainly because
it changes often with code refactoring and new features.

If you are new to translation, start by commenting in the
[NAHPU translation spreadsheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1zG3vkcIgsfPMAal2lai876juXS3U_B4DHPEAxi1-Qko/edit?usp=sharing).
To edit pages directly, follow the
[documentation contribution guide](../../docs) for editing, local development,
and review.

## Where translations live

Each language has its own folder under `src/content/docs/`, named with the
language code: `en`, `pt`, `es`, and `id`. A translated page keeps the same
path as its English source, for example `en/usages/specimens.mdoc` and
`pt/usages/specimens.mdoc`.

Write or update the English page first. Then update each translation so shared
guidance, navigation, and terminology stay aligned across languages.

## Review an existing translation

{% steps %}

1. Open the English page and its translation side by side.

2. Read the translation against the English source and correct mistranslated
   meaning, unnatural phrasing, and terms that differ from the
   [Translation glossary](../glossary).

3. Add yourself to the page's `authors` front matter.

   ```yaml
   ---
   title: "Página traduzida"
   authors:
     - Reviewer Name
   ---
   ```

4. Submit the change for review, as described in the
   [documentation contribution guide](../../docs).

{% /steps %}

Every page under a non-English locale carries an **AI-assisted translation**
notice below its title. The notice is added automatically, so translators do
not write it into the page. Adding `authors` switches the notice from "check
for accuracy" to the reviewed state and names the reviewers. Add `authors` only
after a person has read the translation against the English source and
corrected it. Until then, the unreviewed notice is the honest label. Pages that
fall back to English, such as this Contributing section, do not show it.

## Add a new language

We use AI to assist with the first draft of a new language. If you are
interested, please reach out to one of our [team members](/teams). Our workflow
for adding a new language involves:

1. Creating a new language directory in the `src/content/docs` folder. The
   folder name follows the language code (e.g., `en`, `es`, `fr`).
2. Using a large language model to translate the English documentation,
   following the [Translation glossary](../glossary).
3. Adding the sidebar translations in the `astro.config.mjs` file.
4. Reviewing and revising the AI translation for accuracy by a team member.
5. Adding the reviewer to the page's `authors` front matter, which switches
   the page's translation notice to the reviewed state.

The how-to recipes and Day One guide are also bundled in the NAHPU app, so a
new documentation language is usually added together with an
[app translation](../app).
